WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.220
How can decision answers recover

2
00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.860
a solution? A yes-or-no oracle

3
00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.680
can guide a polynomial number

4
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.320
of smaller decisions until an

5
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:10.800
actual witness is exposed. We

6
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.220
will recover an independent set,

7
00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.000
then distinguish witness search from

8
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.320
optimum-value search and from a

9
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.540
one-output polynomial reduction.

10
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:22.640
Start with a graph and

11
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.840
a requested independent-set size k.

12
00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.120
First ask whether the original

13
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.260
instance is feasible; if not,

14
00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:31.440
report failure rather than trying

15
00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.140
to construct an impossible witness.

16
00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.060
If feasible, test deleting a

17
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:37.640
chosen vertex while keeping the

18
00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:38.680
same target size.

19
00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:40.940
If deleting the chosen vertex

20
00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.240
preserves feasibility for the target

21
00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.920
size, discard that vertex. Otherwise

22
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:47.540
every solution meeting the target

23
00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:49.120
must contain it, so add

24
00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:50.020
it to the answer and

25
00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:51.140
delete it together with its

26
00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:52.980
neighbours. Reduce the target by

27
00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.360
one and continue on the

28
00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:55.100
remaining graph.

29
00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:58.180
On the five-vertex path, an

30
00:00:58.180 --> 00:00:59.800
independent set of size three

31
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.000
is one, three and five.

32
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.420
Deleting vertex one leaves a

33
00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:07.240
four-vertex path with maximum independent-set

34
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.560
size two, so vertex one

35
00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:10.960
is forced for this target.

36
00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.020
Commit one and remove its

37
00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:14.760
neighbour two, leaving the path

38
00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.380
three, four, five with target

39
00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:17.780
two.

40
00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.300
In that residual path, deleting

41
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:21.720
three would leave the single

42
00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.180
edge four-five, which cannot supply

43
00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.820
two independent vertices. Commit three,

44
00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.220
remove four, then choose the

45
00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:31.800
remaining vertex five. The accumulated

46
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.540
witness one, three, five has

47
00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:36.320
no internal edge and reaches

48
00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:37.480
the requested size.

49
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:39.560
The invariant is that the

50
00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.700
current graph contains an independent

51
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.520
set of the remaining requested

52
00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.940
size compatible with every committed

53
00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:48.420
vertex. A discard preserves it

54
00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.240
by the oracle answer, and

55
00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.080
a forced commitment preserves it

56
00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.960
by removing exactly the forbidden

57
00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:57.000
neighbours. Each iteration removes at

58
00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.080
least one vertex, so at

59
00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.440
most linearly many oracle decisions

60
00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.100
are needed after the initial

61
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:03.460
check.

62
00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.060
To find the maximum size,

63
00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.080
use the monotone threshold predicate

64
00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:10.600
before constructing a witness. Maintain

65
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.280
a feasible lower bound zero

66
00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:14.640
and infeasible upper bound one

67
00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:15.700
more than the number of

68
00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.940
vertices, query the midpoint and

69
00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.180
update the appropriate side. When

70
00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:21.440
the bounds differ by one,

71
00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:23.200
the feasible bound is the

72
00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.260
optimum, after logarithmically many threshold

73
00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:26.740
calls.

74
00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.660
Simply querying n, n divided

75
00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:31.340
by two, n divided by

76
00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:33.080
four and so on does

77
00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.100
not locate every possible optimum.

78
00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:37.640
A correct binary search retains

79
00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.260
both bounds and chooses the

80
00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:40.800
next midpoint from the current

81
00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:43.440
interval. Once the optimum size

82
00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.840
is known, the delete-or-commit procedure

83
00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.280
recovers a witness of that

84
00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:47.780
size.

85
00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.400
The factoring tutorial uses the

86
00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.040
same decision-to-search principle for integers

87
00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.560
at least two. Ask whether

88
00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.960
a nontrivial factor exists below

89
00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:00.540
a threshold, then binary-search the

90
00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:02.760
least feasible threshold if the

91
00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.260
square-root threshold is feasible. A

92
00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:07.620
factor is verified by divisibility;

93
00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:09.100
a no answer at the

94
00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.580
square-root threshold identifies a prime

95
00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:12.860
in that input domain.

96
00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:14.960
Things to take note of.

97
00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:17.520
Check initial feasibility, state the

98
00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.720
residual invariant, and count oracle

99
00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:22.620
calls plus transformation work. Recovering

100
00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.040
an optimum number is not

101
00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.500
automatically recovering its witness, and

102
00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:28.500
a multi-query search construction is

103
00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:29.820
not the same object as

104
00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:31.620
a single-instance Karp reduction.
